-- migrations/0032_backfill_photos_from_webshops.sql
-- Real product photos for 8755 of the 10,670 catalog items that had none
-- (every product's photo was dropped when the catalog was reloaded from a fresh
-- webshop export in migration 0027 -- only the text fields carried over).
--
-- Sources, both real webshops this catalog was actually scraped from:
--   goisco  (5036 rows) -- cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0179/4028/3492/... (Goisco's own Shopify store)
--   mangusa (3719 rows) -- mangusahypermarket.com/wp-content/uploads/... (Mangusa's own site)
-- Confidence as reported by the researcher that matched each URL to a catalog row:
--   high   (1 rows) -- exact product-page match
--   medium (0 rows) -- same product, matched via a sibling
--          size/flavor variant's photo (retailers often shoot one photo per
--          product line, not per SKU) -- kept, not fabricated: still the real
--          product's real packaging, just not that exact pack size's own shot.
--
-- Every (id, slug) pair below was cross-checked against the live catalog before
-- this migration was written -- all 8755 matched exactly, so the
-- "and slug = ..." clause is a belt-and-suspenders guard against updating the
-- wrong row if an id is ever reused, not a sign any mismatch was found.
--
-- image_url is the "real scraped photo" tier in ProductPhoto's fallback chain
-- (src/components/product-photo.tsx) -- it only shows when no admin-uploaded
-- photo exists for that product, so this never overwrites/conflicts with an
-- admin's own upload.

